Three edits to this one file.

(a) Fetch re-arm. Hoist the baseURL template literal out of the beforeAll at line 16 to a module-scope const declared above the beforeAll, leaving beforeAll as `patchFetchForSqlJS(baseURL); return init({ baseURL });`. Add a top-level beforeEach directly after the beforeAll that calls patchFetchForSqlJS(baseURL), preceded by a comment saying: the afterEach's vi.restoreAllMocks() is file-scoped rather than spy-scoped, so it also takes down the global.fetch spy this file's beforeAll installed; nothing breaks today because init() has already run and sql.js caches the compiled wasm, but from the second test on global.fetch is the real jsdom fetch; patchFetchForSqlJS is a vi.spyOn(...).mockImplementation(...) with no internal restore, so calling it again is idempotent. Leave the afterEach at line 35 exactly as it is — the fix re-arms the patch, it does not narrow the restore.

(b) Rewrite the comment at lines 28-31 (the block beginning 'Teardown lives here rather than at the tail of a test body' and ending 'restore mocks, then close handles'). It currently over-claims on both halves. Replace it with this text, keeping the file's existing wrapping style and its habit of naming tests by title rather than by position:
  // Teardown lives here rather than at the tail of a test body: an assertion
  // failing above it would skip the cleanup. What survives that is narrower
  // than it looks, so each half is scoped to what it actually covers.
  //
  // vi.restoreAllMocks() restores every spy in the file. Five of the nine
  // tests mock console.log — the three transaction tests, the once-per-pattern
  // test and the once-per-handle test — and each of those restores its own spy
  // at the tail of its body anyway; this is the backstop for an assertion that
  // fails before it gets there. The other four never mock console.log, so for
  // them there is nothing to restore.
  //
  // The loop closes the handles that were pushed into it, and exactly one test
  // pushes any: 'should report an unparseable pattern once per database
  // handle, not once per process'. The other eight call openDatabase() without
  // registering the handle, so their handles are untouched either way.
  //
  // Mirrors ./index.electron.test.ts on both halves — restore mocks, then
  // close handles — where every test does register both.
  Requirements on this comment: it must not say 'every test' or 'every handle'; it must not attribute the handle claim to the test opening at line 381 ('should still match a valid regex that does match, and not throw on one that does not'), which never pushes; it must not call any test 'the last test' or 'the final test', because the handle-registering one is the eighth of nine. Name tests by title.

(c) Append a final it(...) at the end of the describe block, named 'keeps the sql.js wasm fetch patched for every test, not just the first', whose body is a single `expect(vi.isMockFunction(globalThis.fetch)).toBe(true)`. Precede it with a comment saying it is deliberately last and why: a fetch assertion placed first would still see the beforeAll's patch, because no afterEach has run yet — verified on this checkout, where a first-positioned probe prints isMock=true with the defect present and a last-positioned probe prints isMock=false — so it would pass for the wrong reason and be vacuous under -t filtering. This mirrors the convention argued at index.electron.test.ts:129-137.
